\begin{note}
Quicksort is famous for having an $O(n^2)$ worst case runtime,
but also usually being faster than merge sort.
This is for two reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The worst case runtime rarely happens.
    When the input list is randomly arranged,
    quicksort has average runtime $O(n\log n)$ which maches merge sort.
\item quicksort has a small \emph{constant factor}.
\end{enumerate}
Average case runtime analysis requires tracking probabilities and is beyond the scope of this class.
\end{note}
